fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1092", description: some(value: "A DICTIONARY-DIRECTED DISPATCH ONTO A RULE-DEFINED OPERATION SILENTLY ANSWERS FALSE, where the value-directed route on the same operation answers correctly. LIVE ON MAIN, no op-scoped requires involved — found while scoping WI-1091, which merely walked into it.\n\nMEASURED, one program, two routes. sort Color declares operation ceq(a: Color, b: Color) -> Bool defined ONLY by <=> rules (ceq(red,red) <=> true, ceq(red,blue) <=> false, ...) and provides PartialEq[T = Color, eq = ceq]. A generic sort Holder { sort HT = ?; requires PartialEq[T = HT]; operation same(a: HT, b: HT) -> Bool = PartialEq.eq(a, b) }. A driver asks: if Holder.same(red(), red()) then 1 else 0. DICTIONARY ROUTE gives Ok(Int(0)). CONTROL, the same eq reached with CONCRETE arguments and no dictionary — if PartialEq.eq(red(), red()) then 1 else 0 — gives Ok(Int(1)). Both load clean; the wrong one raises nothing. Dropping the requires is NOT an available control: WI-325 refuses the abstract call, so the dictionary is not optional on that spelling.\n\nMECHANISM, traced not inferred. The deferred dispatch resolves CORRECTLY — the sort_ops table honours the provision op binding and hands back Color.ceq — but ceq has no runnable body, so entering it decides nothing and the call yields false (the SLD bridge residualizes). VALUE-DIRECTION never reaches ceq at all: it leaves the call as the SPEC op PartialEq.eq, which eval eq_bridge_target recognises and prove_rule_predicate_value discharges by ordinary SLD. So the dictionary route is MORE PRECISE about the target and thereby LOSES the discharge that makes a rule-defined override work.\n\nTHE GAP IS GENERAL, not about eq. reduce_apply gives a SPELLED functor the relation treatment (if self.kb.cites_a_relation(functor) then start_relation_apply), and dispatch_resolved_operation — reached by every dictionary-threaded and OpRef dispatch — does not; its WI-818 fall-through classifies such a target through unrunnable_target_error instead. A rule-defined operation is therefore runnable when NAMED and not when DISPATCHED. WI-818 own doc says that fall-through exists so the host-entry path and the in-body path report the SAME verdict for the same target; this is the third path, and it disagrees with both.\n\nFIX DIRECTION: give dispatch_resolved_operation the same relation / rule-predicate arm reduce_apply has, ahead of unrunnable_target_error, and re-derive WI-818 classifier rather than inheriting it. op_is_interpretable currently answers is_interpreter_mapped_op || op_has_runnable_body, which is what makes a claused-but-bodyless target look unrunnable, and it is also read by the supplier filter — check spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier before widening it.\n\nWHY NOT WI-580: that ticket is Delivered and its own route (value-direction through the eq-bridge) still works — its test is green. WHY NOT WI-1091: that ticket needs this fixed FIRST (widening the op-scoped deferral routes List.member eq through the dictionary and reproduces this as member(red, [red]) = false), but the defect exists without it.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the two-route program above computes 1 on BOTH routes, with the control asserted in the SAME test so a fix that breaks value-direction cannot pass; push_choice_test wi580_member_over_rule_defined_eq_decides stays green; a stated verdict for a target that is neither body-backed, host-mapped, nor claused (still unrunnable, still loud); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-13T12:32:29Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1092", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED, BUT THE FILED MECHANISM WAS WRONG — re-measured before building on it, and every claim below is from a run, not from reading the source.\n\nWHAT THE TICKET SAID: a rule-defined operation is runnable when NAMED and not when DISPATCHED; `dispatch_resolved_operation` lacks `reduce_apply`'s relation arm and dies in WI-818's fall-through. MEASURED FALSE, four `eprintln`s, one run. The dictionary route DOES reach the eq bridge; the bridge DOES fire (`eq_bridge_target` returns `Color.ceq` for both operands); `prove_rule_predicate` comes back Refuted — from a candidate set with ZERO clauses. The relation arm the ticket asked for could not have fired on either route anyway: `cites_a_relation` is false for an operation symbol whatever clauses it owns (it tests the Goal/Rule/EquationFunctor kinds), so no `reduce_apply` asymmetry exists to mirror.\n\nTHE REAL DEFECT. `rule ceq(red, red) <=> true` indexes its clause under the CONNECTIVE (WI-898), so `ceq` owns none. A DECLARED operation with no clause, no body, no host mapping and no builtin reached the resolver anyway, exhausted the empty candidate set, and every caller rendered `Refuted` as `Bool(false)`. Four sites did it: `dispatch_resolved_operation`'s eq-bridge arm, the host-entry twin in `eval/mod.rs`, the `eq` builtin's carrier dispatch, and the resolver's own `sem_eq_dispatch`.\n\nFIX: an absent definition is not a false one. `PredicateProof::Undefined`, decided at the ONE site that knows the search had no clause to try (`prove_rule_predicate`), gated by a new `op_info::declared_op_with_no_definition` that composes the four backing questions rather than widening any of them (`op_is_interpretable` is left alone — the supplier filter reads it, per the ticket's own warning). The eval faces render it as WI-818's `OperationBodyMissing`; the resolver, which has no error channel, leaves the goal undecided rather than deciding it wrongly. An UNDECLARED symbol with no clauses still refutes — that is closed-world SLD, and `resolve.rs`'s `no_such_pred` / `myeq0` unit tests pin it; DECLARED is what makes the difference, because a declaration promises an implementation and a bare name does not.\n\nBOTH EXISTING CONTROLS WERE VACUOUS, WHICH IS HOW THIS SURVIVED. The filed test's control (`PartialEq.eq(red(), red())` = 1) never consulted `ceq`: two IDENTICAL operands take a structural reflexivity fast path before any carrier dispatch. `wi580_member_over_rule_defined_eq_decides`, green since WI-580 and named for this exact capability, was the same — its three rows were answered by the structural path and by an empty search read as refutation, never by the `<=>` rules it is written around. A fixture whose custom `eq` AGREES with structural equality measures nothing. The rewritten tests invert it: `rule ceq(red, blue)` with no `ceq(red, red)`, so consulting the clauses and not consulting them give OPPOSITE answers on every row. WI-580's fixture moved to predicate heads (`rule ceq(red, red)`), which is what it always meant; its `blue_in_rb` row is the one assertion there that a definition-less `ceq` fails, and the site says so.\n\nSPEC (user decision: \"loud + correct the spec\", not the `<=> true` -> predicate-clause lowering). Two false claims in kernel-language.md, both measured:\n  * §5.3 advertised `rule eq(red, red) <=> true` as \"how a carrier defines an operation by cases (the `eq` family only)\". Nothing reads such an equation. Corrected to the predicate-head spelling, with the WI-1092 verdict added to the WI-881 paragraph. The same sentence's other half — `rule neq(?a,?b) <=> not(eq(?a,?b))` as \"how the prelude defines a standalone derived operation\" — is false too: `PartialEq.neq` is a registered resolver builtin and a builtin decides its goal before any clause is consulted.\n  * §8.7's spec-satisfaction example (`fact Eq[T = Color]` + namespace-level `rule eq(...) <=> ...`) does nothing at all in EITHER spelling — measured with an inverted fixture: with no member bound as the carrier's `eq`, nothing dispatches and structural equality answers. Rewritten to the shape that works: an `operation ceq` member, predicate-head clauses under it, and `provides PartialEq[T = Color, eq = ceq]` (or `fact PartialEq[T = Color, eq = Color.ceq]` from outside the sort). Also states that there is no catch-all `<=> false` case to write beside them — absence is falsity over a complete search.\n\n`EvalError::OperationBodyMissing`'s message said \"this is a typer-guaranteed invariant violation (should be unreachable)\". This change makes an ordinary authoring mistake reach it, so it now names the four things looked for and the equation trap. WI-1008's header quote of the old sentence was updated with it.\n\nFULL WORKSPACE GREEN: 29 binaries, 4646 tests, 0 failures. WHICH TESTS FAIL WHEN THE FIX IS BACKED OUT — measured, by running the suite before the change: `wi1092_dispatched_rule_defined_target_test::a_declared_but_undefined_eq_is_loud_on_every_route` (all six rows returned Ok(Int(0))) and `wi580_member_over_rule_defined_eq_decides`'s `blue_in_rb` row. The other WI-1092 test, `a_rule_defined_eq_decides_the_dictionary_route_and_the_named_one`, passes EITHER WAY by design and says so at its site: it is the regression guard for the half the fix must not break.\n\nMIGRATION — this is a user-visible behaviour break, and deliberately so. A knowledge base written to the previously documented form (a carrier `eq` given by untagged `<=>` equations) used to load and answer `false`; it now loads and raises `OperationBodyMissing` at the first comparison that reaches the carrier's `eq`. The repair is one line per case: drop the `<=> true` and keep the head (`rule ceq(red, red)`), and delete any `<=> false` catch-all — absence is falsity. Nothing in the stdlib or the corpus was written that way; the two fixtures that were are the two updated here.\n\nFOR WI-1091, and it changes that ticket's own plan. Its fifth increment is filed as \"a body-less dispatch target that HAS clauses must be discharged by SLD rather than reaching `unrunnable_target_error`\", derived from `member(red, [red]) = false` under the widened placement. MEASURED TODAY: a body-less target that has clauses ALREADY is discharged by SLD, on the dictionary route, through the eq bridge — the increment as filed has nothing to implement, because `test.wi580ce.Color.ceq` never had clauses. Its fixture now does (predicate heads), so re-measure the widened placement against it before writing any of that increment; and if the silent-false recurs there, it will now be a raise instead, which names the target.\n\nREVIEW: `/code-review` high, run on the diff. ONE finding on this change — `declared_op_with_no_definition` ran on every `prove_rule_predicate` call, and its slow tier scans every `OperationInfo` fact when the operation has no cached record. FIXED by moving the probe to the REFUTATION EDGE: the two verdicts differ only where the search came back empty, so a proved compare on the per-dispatch `eq` path now pays nothing, and `operation_is_declared`'s scan lost its per-probe Vec allocation (`rules_by_functor_iter`). The review's other four findings are on WI-822 LEG 1's committed code rather than this diff, and all four are APPLIED INLINE HERE — one as a fix, three as verdicts recorded at their sites, because verifying them refuted the defect each described. ONE FIX: req_insertion's `chain_for` took `op_dict_entries(op)` whenever an `enclosing_op` was recorded, while the producer (`TypingEnv::set_enclosing_op`) installs the composed chain only when the operation DECLARES an op-scoped one and otherwise keeps the sort chain it was given — so the consumer re-derived its sort half from the op (`impl_parent_of_op`) where the producer read the recorded `enclosing_sort`. It now mirrors that branch, which is the only way the two cannot drift. THREE REFUTED, each with the derivation written at the site so the next reader does not redo it: (a) `push_op_scoped_slots`' `built_for != target` raise — the proposed reachable shape (a `provides Spec[…, member = other]` redirect) needs an op half AND a redirect, and the route that redirects is the DEFERRED one, which carries no op half, while a provision binding rivalling a carrier's own member is a supplier tie refused before it runs; examined, not driven, and the site now names the policy to adopt if a fixture ever reaches it. (b) The `__req_self` asymmetry in `resolve_bridge_requirements` is real and benign: the difference between the two frame shapes is only ever an ADDED self slot, which at `sort_len == 0` is arity 0 — `Dictionary(impl: parent)`, the frame's own sort — and strictly better than the absent slot's raise-at-the-read; making it uniform re-introduces the pre-widening regression that branch exists to avoid. (c) Two op slots CAN mint one `_o<id>` name, but only by sharing a base and a hash-cons id, and a shared id is structural identity — the same requirement twice, which substitutes to one goal and one dictionary, so first-wins hands the second slot exactly what resolving it again would produce.", at: "2026-08-13T12:32:19Z")
